1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,984
One company sold tiny slices of itself, for $135 each.

2
00:00:06,134 --> 00:00:08,720
It raised $75 billion.

3
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:11,478
In three days, the stock jumped two-thirds.

4
00:00:11,628 --> 00:00:14,044
Then, it lost over half its value.

5
00:00:14,444 --> 00:00:19,433
This is the story of the SpaceX IPO, the biggest the world had ever seen.

6
00:00:19,733 --> 00:00:23,289
And the wildest part? The next giants are already lining up.

7
00:00:25,339 --> 00:00:27,862
Imagine a company is a giant pizza.

8
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:30,119
Every share is one tiny slice.

9
00:00:30,419 --> 00:00:38,307
For 24 years, SpaceX only sold its slices to a private club: founders, employees, and big investors.

10
00:00:38,557 --> 00:00:40,634
Everyone else was stuck behind the rope.

11
00:00:40,984 --> 00:00:45,927
An IPO, or initial public offering, is the day the doors swing open.

12
00:00:46,177 --> 00:00:51,223
The company sells slices to anyone, on a public stock market, like the Nasdaq.

13
00:00:51,573 --> 00:00:53,340
Initial: it's the first time.

14
00:00:53,490 --> 00:00:55,127
Public: anyone can buy.

15
00:00:55,277 --> 00:00:57,051
Offering: the company is selling.

16
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:00,048
So why would a company open its doors at all?

17
00:01:00,318 --> 00:01:05,426
Reason one: money. Selling new slices brings in cash to build things.

18
00:01:05,696 --> 00:01:09,443
Reason two: the private club finally gets a way to sell, too.

19
00:01:09,713 --> 00:01:11,215
That's called cashing out.

20
00:01:11,485 --> 00:01:13,729
And SpaceX needed a lot of cash.

21
00:01:13,929 --> 00:01:23,717
For its rockets, its Starlink satellites, and, after merging with Elon Musk's AI company, ex AI, in February, giant AI computers.

22
00:01:23,987 --> 00:01:31,401
In 2025, it brought in about $18.7 billion, but still lost nearly $5 billion.

23
00:01:31,671 --> 00:01:38,368
Think of a lemonade stand that sells a lot of lemonade, but spends even more building a giant new factory.

24
00:01:38,638 --> 00:01:40,601
Building the future is expensive.

25
00:01:42,651 --> 00:01:46,383
Going public is like launching a rocket. There's a countdown.

26
00:01:46,653 --> 00:01:48,226
Step one: the paperwork.

27
00:01:48,426 --> 00:01:53,562
SpaceX sent the regulator, the SEC, a secret draft on April 1.

28
00:01:53,832 --> 00:01:56,883
On May 20, it made that document public.

29
00:01:57,153 --> 00:02:04,000
It's called an S-1, and it's basically the company's diary: what it earns, what it owes, and what could go wrong.

30
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:07,911
And anyone can read it for free, on the SEC's website.

31
00:02:08,311 --> 00:02:09,784
Step two: the roadshow.

32
00:02:09,984 --> 00:02:15,227
Starting June 4, SpaceX and its banks pitched the stock to huge investors.

33
00:02:15,497 --> 00:02:18,545
The lead banks were Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley.

34
00:02:18,815 --> 00:02:24,198
Banks like these are called underwriters. They organize the sale, and find the buyers.

35
00:02:24,468 --> 00:02:28,995
For that work, the banks get paid a fee: a small slice of the money raised.

36
00:02:29,395 --> 00:02:30,950
Step three: the order book.

37
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:35,372
Investors write down how many slices they want, and at what price.

38
00:02:35,642 --> 00:02:41,089
Normally, a company names a price range. If the book overflows, the price can rise.

39
00:02:41,359 --> 00:02:47,854
SpaceX skipped the range, and named one fixed price: $135 a share.

40
00:02:48,124 --> 00:02:52,969
Even so, big investors asked for about four times more shares than there were.

41
00:02:53,369 --> 00:02:57,759
Step four: pricing night. On June 11, the deal was locked in.

42
00:02:58,029 --> 00:03:02,530
SpaceX sold about 556 million new shares.

43
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:10,877
$135, times 556 million, is about $75 billion.

44
00:03:11,227 --> 00:03:17,502
The old record belonged to Saudi Aramco: about $29 billion, in 2019.

45
00:03:17,772 --> 00:03:21,398
SpaceX's deal was more than two and a half times bigger.

46
00:03:21,798 --> 00:03:25,459
And this time, regular people got a real seat at the table.

47
00:03:25,729 --> 00:03:29,985
Usually, everyday investors get 5-10% of an IPO

48
00:03:30,255 --> 00:03:39,190
SpaceX planned up to 30%, and in the end gave about a fifth to retail buyers, through apps like Robinhood, Fidelity, and Schwab.

49
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:41,747
There was one more trick: the greenshoe.

50
00:03:42,017 --> 00:03:46,573
It lets the banks sell 15% extra shares, if demand is hot.

51
00:03:46,843 --> 00:03:53,760
Demand was hot. The banks used it, and the total grew to about $85.7 billion.

52
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:59,556
One more detail matters for later: Elon Musk kept about four-fifths of the votes.

53
00:03:59,826 --> 00:04:03,831
The public could own slices, but Elon still picks the toppings.

54
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:09,114
June 12, 2026. The opening bell.

55
00:04:09,384 --> 00:04:15,199
SPCX opened at $150, already above the IPO price.

56
00:04:15,469 --> 00:04:24,517
It climbed as high as $176 that day, and closed at $160.95, up 19%.

57
00:04:24,787 --> 00:04:27,941
That made SpaceX worth over $2 trillion.

58
00:04:28,211 --> 00:04:31,678
Headlines called Elon Musk the world's first trillionaire.

59
00:04:32,078 --> 00:04:35,223
A first-day jump like this is called an IPO pop.

60
00:04:35,493 --> 00:04:39,765
Why does it happen? The IPO price is set before trading starts.

61
00:04:40,035 --> 00:04:44,801
If more people want the stock than there are shares to sell, buyers bid the price up.

62
00:04:45,071 --> 00:04:49,060
Some say a big pop means the company left money on the table.

63
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:52,598
In other words, maybe it sold its slices too cheaply.

64
00:04:52,998 --> 00:04:54,724
But the rocket kept climbing.

65
00:04:54,994 --> 00:05:03,248
On June 16, just the third day of trading, SPCX touched $225.64.

66
00:05:03,518 --> 00:05:07,733
That's two-thirds above the IPO price, in three trading days.

67
00:05:08,003 --> 00:05:10,196
Early buyers felt like geniuses.

68
00:05:10,646 --> 00:05:12,237
And then, gravity showed up.

69
00:05:14,287 --> 00:05:19,980
Stocks rarely fall for just one reason. SPCX was pulled down by four weights.

70
00:05:20,330 --> 00:05:22,406
Weight number one: the Cursor deal.

71
00:05:22,606 --> 00:05:30,863
On the same day as the peak, SpaceX agreed to buy the AI coding company Cursor for $60 billion, paid in stock.

72
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:34,893
Paying in stock means cutting new slices from the same pizza.

73
00:05:35,163 --> 00:05:39,281
Every existing slice gets a little smaller. That's called dilution.

74
00:05:39,551 --> 00:05:45,671
Within two trading days, SpaceX lost more than $600 billion in market value.

75
00:05:46,071 --> 00:05:47,964
Weight number two: the lock-up.

76
00:05:48,164 --> 00:05:53,447
Insiders who owned shares before the IPO usually can't sell them for months.

77
00:05:53,717 --> 00:05:57,892
It's a promise that stops everyone from rushing for the exit on day one.

78
00:05:58,162 --> 00:06:04,900
SpaceX's first big unlock was set for August 6: about 911 million shares.

79
00:06:05,170 --> 00:06:08,189
That was almost 7% of all SpaceX shares.

80
00:06:08,459 --> 00:06:12,927
Worried about a flood of selling, investors pushed the price down ahead of time.

81
00:06:13,197 --> 00:06:18,932
On July 27, the stock hit a record low, under $109.

82
00:06:19,202 --> 00:06:24,119
The funny part? On unlock day itself, the stock actually rose 6%.

83
00:06:24,389 --> 00:06:28,796
And over the next five trading days, it climbed about 35%.

84
00:06:29,146 --> 00:06:31,315
The fear did more damage than the flood.

85
00:06:31,865 --> 00:06:33,640
Weight number three: spending.

86
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:38,742
In August, SpaceX handed in its first report card as a public company.

87
00:06:39,012 --> 00:06:44,002
Revenue grew 92%, to $7.8 billion. Great!

88
00:06:44,272 --> 00:06:49,556
But it spent $18.4 billion in a single quarter, most of it on AI

89
00:06:49,826 --> 00:06:55,608
That's money for giant computer chips and data centers, the kind that power AI models.

90
00:06:55,878 --> 00:07:01,219
Investors hate surprise bills. The next day, the stock fell more than 10%.

91
00:07:01,489 --> 00:07:10,559
Just before that report, on August 3, SPCX had touched its all-time low: $104.83.

92
00:07:10,829 --> 00:07:14,088
From the peak, that's a fall of about 53%.

93
00:07:14,488 --> 00:07:16,980
Weight number four: the price tag itself.

94
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:23,565
At over $2 trillion, buyers were paying for a future of Mars trips and space data centers.

95
00:07:23,835 --> 00:07:28,075
When a price assumes everything goes right, any bad news hurts more.

96
00:07:28,345 --> 00:07:33,169
It's like a rocket made of glass: beautiful, but one small pebble can crack it.

97
00:07:33,439 --> 00:07:39,946
And there was a hidden risk: in the third quarter, one AI customer made up more than a third of revenue.

98
00:07:40,216 --> 00:07:48,899
Bloomberg reported that customer is Anthropic, and the contract can be canceled with 90 days' notice from early 2027.

99
00:07:49,299 --> 00:07:51,488
To be fair, the business kept growing.

100
00:07:51,758 --> 00:07:59,992
In October, revenue jumped 161%, and SpaceX made a $4.4 billion profit.

101
00:08:00,262 --> 00:08:07,743
The stock bounced. But by late November, it was back below its $135 IPO price.

102
00:08:08,193 --> 00:08:12,048
A great company and a great stock are not always the same thing.

103
00:08:14,098 --> 00:08:18,016
Remember the queue at the launch pad? The next giants are taking off.

104
00:08:18,286 --> 00:08:26,030
On November 18, Anthropic, the AI company behind Claude, priced its IPO at $83 a share.

105
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:32,283
It raised $81.3 billion, more than SpaceX's original $75 billion.

106
00:08:32,553 --> 00:08:39,303
Its shares jumped on day one, then slid back toward the IPO price within days. Sound familiar?

107
00:08:39,653 --> 00:08:44,289
So is SpaceX still the biggest IPO ever? It depends how you count.

108
00:08:44,559 --> 00:08:51,003
Count SpaceX's greenshoe, and its $85.7 billion is still bigger, at least for now.

109
00:08:51,273 --> 00:08:54,578
Count only the starting deal, and Anthropic wins.

110
00:08:54,978 --> 00:08:58,944
Next in line: Open AI, the maker of ChatGPT

111
00:08:59,214 --> 00:09:07,867
It has reportedly filed in secret, and is aiming for 2027, with reported valuations above $1 trillion.

112
00:09:08,137 --> 00:09:15,721
And other big private companies, like Databricks and Stripe, are often named as future candidates, with no dates set.

113
00:09:16,171 --> 00:09:20,670
So what can the SpaceX story teach us, before the next mega IPO?

114
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:23,564
One: a first-day pop is not a promise.

115
00:09:23,864 --> 00:09:28,878
Two: check the lock-up calendar. More SpaceX shares unlock on December 8.

116
00:09:29,178 --> 00:09:33,167
Three: watch for dilution. New slices make your slice smaller.

117
00:09:33,467 --> 00:09:38,032
Four: read the S-1 diary, especially the part about what could go wrong.

118
00:09:38,332 --> 00:09:41,375
And five: a famous name doesn't make the price cheap.

119
00:09:41,825 --> 00:09:46,201
SpaceX is still building rockets, satellites, and AI computers.

120
00:09:46,471 --> 00:09:49,429
Whether its stock reaches Mars is a different story.

121
00:09:49,779 --> 00:09:54,951
Because in the stock market, just like in space, what goes up has to fight gravity.

122
00:09:55,601 --> 00:09:59,264
This video is for education, not financial advice.

